From 4dfafc5b117e4b27b027a40d50da8793be94fa0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:37 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(viabedrock): retain Bedrock creative catalog entries

Preserve creative groups and exact entry identities so client UI can reflect the connected Bedrock server catalog and duplicate Java fallback items still resolve to the intended Bedrock item. Resync creative slot contents after sending transactions to avoid local ghost items reported by testers.

Request creative stacks through CraftCreative palette IDs, CraftResults,
optional Destroy, and Place actions. Reconcile their authoritative network
IDs before processing queued selections. Preserve queued creative writes
when Java closes its local GUI, while discarding screen-specific clicks.
Use the same container request-slot boundary for armor and offhand slots.

The request sequence matches protocol 2193 and Geyser 2.11.3 build 1247
PlayerInventoryTranslator.translateCreativeRequest. Unit tests check
palette identity, replacement/deletion, equipment destinations, and
acknowledgment ordering across GUI close. No Geyser changes are required.

Open the native player inventory before sending creative requests and wait for its acknowledgement. Preserve writes across delayed OPEN responses and local Java GUI closure. Writes arriving during a pending native CLOSE remain queued and reopen the inventory after the acknowledgement, rather than disappearing between native windows. Packet-handler tests cover rapid writes with distinct slots and quantities during that close.

Retain exact creative entry identity and complete native item snapshots; reject ambiguous same-Java-item fallbacks rather than choosing arbitrary potion/custom variants. Enforce the native creative stack maximum. Catalog and transfer tests cover exact markers, component differences and oversized selections.

Translate Java creative-menu drops from slot -1 into native creative
craft/results/drop actions without replacing held inventory. Java 26.3
bytecode emits this slot for creative-menu Q. Geyser
PlayerInventoryTranslator documents the same native created-output slot
50 flow since Bedrock 1.18.2. Actual packet tests cover plate and stone
counts, pending-close queuing, and invalid stack bounds.

Build the player search source from the current connection's complete
creative registry. Native Player rebuild14020ec60 admits definition
categories one through four in registry order; it does not use group
category, Java fallback identity, stack count or crafting-grid size.
Keep exact entry references, duplicates, variants and source order in a
separate immutable list, alongside the complete creative palette.
ItemRewriter supplies the connection registry on every catalog receipt.

The complete original rebuild and vector growth/cleanup execute 906
controls, including every category byte, absent definitions, duplicates,
allocation boundaries and repeated replacement. Item copy/destruction,
allocation and CRT boundaries are supplied. Text, unlock and craftability
evaluators, native collection population and authored player screen/input
integration remain separate work.

Construct creative groups with the native category/name rules before
assigning entries. Only categories one through five register. Named
groups retain their first declaration within a category; anonymous groups
remain distinct and ignore their supplied icon. Entry group indices address
the registered group vector directly rather than a remapped wire table.

Membership rewrites the shared item definition category in entry order.
The final update controls search admission for every variant of that
definition, and survives subsequent catalog replacement without membership.
Retain immutable group and category sources in group/member order for the
player model while keeping the complete palette and search independent.

Complete pinned registry construction, content replacement, native group
registration, member assignment, definition updates, search rebuild and
registry destruction execute 186 controls. Descriptor conversion, typed
item backing, item copies/destruction, diagnostics and ordinary allocator/
CRT boundaries remain supplied. The frame population reference also
executes 181 original controls across 2,296 frames, including first-match
catalog identity; frame publication, evaluator and player UI integration
remain separate work.

Retain original native group icons beside their Java presentation. Bind
connection catalog snapshots to RecipeCollection with the actual native
identity comparator and an explicit availability evaluator. Resolve each
source stack through the first matching catalog entry before selecting its
group. Each populated row owns a stack snapshot and keeps that catalog
reference through availability refreshes. Require at least two native members and a defined icon, including
icons whose root quantity is zero; compare header identity and quantity
independently of the translated Java fallback.

Complete original content registration, native item equality, first-match
source assignment, collection partition, header construction and projection
execute together in 300 controls and three retained toggle/refresh flows.
Typed packet/descriptor backing, item
copy/destruction, allocator/CRT and injected availability remain supplied
boundaries. Progressive frame delivery, actual availability evaluation and
full player controller/screen integration remain separate work.

Populate the five creative collections through one native frame budget,
visiting search, construction, nature, equipment and items in that order.
Retain group/member/destination cursors and initially invalid source rows.
The original provider leaves combined totals below eight pending; preserve
that guard rather than forcing a minimum budget.

Completion reports once on the call after the final write phase and dirties
only the active tab. Keep inactive dirty tabs and header expansion, and use
the native left-tab mapping separately from population order. Bind the
model through ItemRewriter with explicit per-view availability evaluation.

Complete original creative setup, five collection constructors/destructors,
progressive population, controller tick, weak-owner operations, dirty-tab
refresh dispatch and collection ordering/projection execute in 508 controls
across 9,438 frames. Three additional weak-owner flows remain reference-only.
Typed descriptor/definition backing, item conversion/copies/destruction,
label and receiver interfaces, injected availability, unrelated base tick
and allocator/CRT boundaries remain supplied. Actual availability policy,
full screen ownership and installed-screen frame delivery remain open.

Check creative edits against the shared native permissions before allocating
requests or predicting slot writes. Validate the incoming whole stack with
the destination provider, including armor identity and capacity. Require
source discard permission before destroying the previous stack. New
palette drops retain typed userdata and reject nonzero byte item locks.
Use the shared raw-mode creative predicate; Undefined does not inherit
Creative from the world as Java's display-mode conversion does.

Recheck source permissions after the temporary inventory opens, when its
server snapshot and actor mode can have changed. Keep incoming palette
validation before opening. Regressions exercise refused writes, valid
armor placement, palette drops and response-handler rejection after a mode
change. The actor mode, world mode and catalog are declared cached inputs;
complete Actor delivery, native creative-catalog construction, physical
controller delivery and live server acceptance remain separate evidence.

Bind the five collection buffers to the connection's registered search and
category callbacks in PlayerRecipeModel. Retain raw query bytes and a
selected-output copy. Raw changes clear selection before updating the
installed search policy and refreshing search immediately, without advancing
population or consuming the controller's dirty tabs. Equal raw bytes keep
selection and the existing snapshot, including edits after a NUL that would
otherwise normalize to the same search text.

Capture category grouping at collection construction from Instabuild; keep
search flat. Explicit policy reinstallation captures current search ability
without replacing source rows, grouping or header expansion. Pass effective
filtering and cursor participation explicitly. Bind recipe reads to an
explicit presentation context. Acknowledged workstation contexts retain
their exact inventory owner and opening generation, including when a later
opening reuses the same container object.

Pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1/build51061372/protocol2193 executes 224 transitions
through original collection construction/destruction, stored callbacks,
query setter1434154d0, item comparison/assignment, normalization, complete
refresh143445150 and native recipe counting. Supplied source/model/map and
actor/recipe backing, text/game-type producers, allocator/CRT and stream
interfaces remain explicit. Category construction flags follow initializer
1434360d0, helper143438210 and vtable slot80's Instabuild getter1434358a0.
Full backend registration, controller text delivery and installed player
screen publication remain separate work. No new live screen claim is made.

Construct the retained model for installed E-key openings before their native
acknowledgment. Read genuine cached player sources without fabricating a
current container or granting item-request authority. Keep the same model
when acknowledgment arrives so search/grouping policy capture and selected
output cannot silently move to a later ability state. Retire contexts by
opening token and inventory identity; keep workstation contexts bound to
container generations. A late player close cannot retire a workbench model.

Bind progressive collection ticks to the inventory lifecycle and invalidate
collections when source contents, recipes, unlocks, rules, abilities or actual
profile preferences change. Read the constructed native player-profile defaults before any server options
packet, independently of Java book defaults. Preserve explicit packet zeros. Catalog replacement rebuilds collection
sources while retaining the backend's captured policies, query and selection.

Current 1.26.60.5/protocol2225 instruction mapping identifies derived model
143a6e950, controller14501f680, registration143a6ce70 and initializer143a6eda0.
The controller initializes filtering to true before profile callbacks. This
mapping does not prove construction timing relative to network acknowledgment
or native frame-level population timing. The unpositioned player context
retains the documented fallback crafting tags until an acknowledged anchor
exists. Authored player scene publication and controller edit delivery remain
unfinished; this change does not claim complete installed-screen parity.

Expose recipe output quantity separately from availability and action
admission. The native 1.26.60.5 count wrapper walks candidates in priority
order, retains shared descriptor consumption across candidates and rounds,
and multiplies successful crafts by each candidate's output amount. Read
crafting input, cursor and combined player inventory in native source order.
This count does not exclude a recipe merely because its grid needs a table.

A physical 1.26.60.5 classic-screen capture shows 44 planks from eight logs
and three stripped logs, three tables from ten oak and two birch planks,
and four chests from thirty oak and two birch planks. Shift-clicking the
last example crafts only three chests from the selected candidate, so the
UI total must not become an action quantity. Regression fixtures retain
these observed totals and exercise candidate overlap and live source reads.

Read Creative output counts from the current raw game mode, including
Default inheritance, independently of Instabuild collection policies.
Expose the quantity through the retained player model and preserve its
opening-generation checks. Native cache timing and complete installed recipe admission remain
unfinished work.

Publish the actual retained player/workbench rows and both independent
native layouts with full component previews in bounded, compressed
protocol-2225 snapshots. Bind edits to an opening, acknowledged container
generation, snapshot revision and monotonic command request. Search and
preferences remain read-only while the native opening is pending.

Use retained collection identities for header expansion and recipe
selection; never authorize from the Java preview item. Native Creative
left-click produces the catalog's unit result before taking a full stack
to the empty cursor. Ordinary Survival selection uses the existing grid
placement and reconciliation path. Full native cache timing and exact
background selection remain unfinished.

Export the versioned How to Play graph with the player program. Resolve
its real topic toggles, named view aliases, section titles, translated
paragraphs and Back mappings. A physical 1.26.60.5 E-key Help control opens
Your First Craft, the authored topic at index five. Preserve the graph
through compressed snapshot transport instead of substituting Java help
text. Full installed Help lifetimes and input-glyph parity still require
comparison.

The current Help callback at145067f30 selects How to Play topic5 for
Survival and Encyclopedia topic47 for Creative or inherited Creative.
Those authored selectors are Your First Craft and Creative Mode. Export
both real graphs so a game-mode update cannot remove the graph retained
by an open Help view. Preserve native crafting-output mappings on actual
slot zero for the existing menu event and request path.

Export Help navigation translations derived from authored section and
category variables, including their nested controls. Literal text-key
scanning misses those concatenated keys, leaving topic/category names
untranslated after program transport even when paragraphs and titles work.
The translated scene round trip covers both navigation and styled text.

Allow installed player presenters to request local recipe-grid windows.
Keep the complete retained collection and all source/action identities in
the snapshot. The initial one-cell view establishes authored geometry;
subsequent ranges follow the real viewport rather than removing catalog
rows or changing search results. Other callers keep full expansion.
A 2,000-cell Creative source regression checks distant ranges, actual menu
slots and exported-program replay without replacing the source model.

Keep Creative catalog cells active independently of recipe ingredients.
Bedrock 1.26.60.5 background callback 14506bb30 bypasses the disabled
recipe gate in Creative mode. A matching protocol-2225 native capture
selects an unavailable trapped-chest recipe through CraftCreative, then
takes its stack to the cursor. Applying the collection's recipe-disabled
flag to published Creative cells colored every uncraftable entry red and
rejected the click before it could reach that request path.

Use the current effective raw game mode for cell permission while leaving
retained availability, captured search policies, row identities and source
counts intact. A snapshot regression checks Creative cells with no recipe
ingredients and restores recipe gating after returning to Survival.

Admit Creative catalog picks with a cursor copy sized to the requested
stack before checking the destination. A unit catalog sample cannot
authorize a 64-item transfer because Container validates the whole source
quantity. Live protocol-2225 tracing reaches core with a current opening
and idle request tracker, then rejects precisely this count mismatch.
Keep the unit result for CraftCreative/CraftResults and use the separate
full stack only for destination admission and cursor prediction. This
preserves the official 1.26.60.5 captured unit-result/full-Take flow.
A request-boundary regression checks stack sizes one and 64, the packet
actions, cursor prediction and its pre-request restoration snapshot.

Supply player-inventory tab and layout variables from the protocol-2225 enums. The 1.26.60 inventory graph references these controller-owned indices, just as cooking graphs reference their own indices. Without them, forced toggle indices resolve to null and mouse selection sends zero, leaving an unselected profile unable to choose Search or change layouts. Keep these authored indices separate from the player profile values.

The original 1.26.60.5 player constructor initializes inventory profile fields
to Search5/Crafting2/filteringfalse/layout0/layout0 at 1402b5b86..1402b5ba9.
The named NBT loader and SetPlayerInventoryOptionsPacket307 producer bind
those fields to the same controller reads. Model creation must use the
constructed profile rather than retain the controller's earlier transient
filter flag. Later packets, including explicit zero tabs, replace the profile while
retaining the model, query and selection. Filtering changes reinstall the
existing policy callbacks; tab-only updates preserve their captured state.
A regression opens without an options packet and observes the populated
Search source, then applies an explicit zero-tab/filter update.

Match official Bedrock 1.26.60.5 primary catalog clicks with an occupied
cursor. Native local-world controls clear a full held stack for both the
same and a different catalog item, leave inventory empty, and pick the new
stack only on the next click. Send one Destroy for the actual cursor
network ID, retain native discard admission, and snapshot the cursor before
prediction so rejection restores its identity and count. This observation
establishes visible behavior, not original native request-byte parity.

Bind Creative secondary clicks and auto-place to official 1.26.60.5
local-world controls. Secondary takes one item into an empty cursor or
discards one held item regardless of the selected catalog identity.
Auto-place preserves the cursor, merges across the combined inventory
before filling empty hotbar then main-inventory slots, and stops at the
available capacity. A full inventory accepts the input without a request.
Snapshot all affected slots before prediction; refusal restores counts
and confirmed identities. Native visible controls establish behavior,
not original native request-byte equality.

Ignore unchanged Java Creative slot echoes before opening or writing the
native inventory. Installed Java 26.3 reopening otherwise issues two
CraftCreative/Destroy/Place replacements for unchanged 64-rail stacks and
replaces their confirmed network IDs. Compare counts and complete native
wire snapshots, including NBT, block state, restrictions and blocking
ticks, while excluding transient network identity. Genuine count or native
user-data changes retain replacement requests. Preserve drop permission,
foreign-container rejection and pending-close ownership checks.

Support the Creative catalog-only toolbar's container_auto_destroy action.
Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 local-world controls discard the
entire selected hotbar stack on Shift-left and Shift-right, preserve the
held cursor, and leave empty slots unchanged. The same Shift input moves
the stack to main inventory in the inventory-only layout. The matching
inventory_screen.json creative_hotbar_container_slot_button overrides
button_auto_place with button.container_auto_destroy.

Advertise this destructive command on a separate optional channel. Bind it
to the current opening, generation and revision, require Creative toolbar
layout and confirmed discard permission, and predict only the selected
permanent hotbar slot. Preserve cursor and inventory snapshots for rejected
requests. These native controls establish visible behavior, not equality
with original-client request bytes.

Plan Survival catalog bulk crafting from the first eligible native ingredient
allocation round, capped at one output stack. Exclude the carried stack from
consumption. Emit concrete CraftRecipeAuto descriptors, CraftResults,
ordered Consume and Place actions, and cursor Drop/Take overflow. Resolve
subsequent references to any mutated slot through the current request ID.
Snapshot the grid, player inventory and cursor before prediction so rejected
requests restore every affected collection.

Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5 and BDS 1.26.60.6 protocol2225 captures establish
16logs-to64planks, one nonstackable sword, repeated source references,
split placement and cursor replacement. With one free inventory item-space,
native places one plank, drops an incompatible/full held stack and takes63.
Initial capacity is checked before consumption: a full inventory with an
incompatible cursor refuses even when consumption would free a source slot.
An empty cursor admits that same craft and output uses the freed source slot.
Targeted request/state tests preserve these boundaries and rejection rollback.

Build ordinary auto-craft ingredients from their retained resolved recipe
descriptors, including wildcard auxiliary 32767. Resolve tag/Molang inputs
from the selected source instead. Native protocol2225 CraftingData declares
oak_log and stick as named wildcard ingredients, but wooden-sword planks
as tags. The official client emits32767 for the former and concrete0 for
planks. A direct installed control exposed source-only metadata0; BDS
accepted it, but acceptance did not establish native request parity.
A real registered-recipe/item-registry regression now preserves both paths.

Read all shaped/shapeless outputs from CraftingData, including returned
buckets and bottles, instead of maintaining Java remainder mappings.
Official 1.26.60.5/protocol2225 controls select secondary output1 before
primary0 with Create actions. Select each created stack chunk separately,
restart inventory merging for each chunk, and use the current request ID
for destinations changed by consumption or earlier output placement.

Six cakes return18 buckets as stacks16+2 without reducing craft count.
With15 existing buckets, the second chunk merges into the first newly
created stack before using another freed slot. Honey-block controls show
returned bottles using the consumed source slot, while the primary output
falls back to the cursor. When all inventory slots remain occupied, drop
secondary output directly from CreatedOutput50 with randomly=false.
Preserve single-output requests without Create actions and snapshot every
predicted grid, inventory and cursor mutation for rejection rollback.

Native capture and byte fixtures cover two-output cake and honey recipes.
Broader recipe families, more than two outputs, live rejection controls and
complete screen parity remain separate validation work.

Retain the recipe query across player and workbench openings within the
connection. Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5/BDS 1.26.60.6 controls on protocol
2225 retain honey-block text after complete workbench closure and inherit
the earlier oak-planks query on the next player-inventory opening.

Copy the last model's raw query before retiring it, then seed the fresh
model with that query. Keep selected output, collections, acknowledgment,
container generation and action sequences scoped to their existing opening.
An explicit empty query remains empty after reopening. Retired models
cannot alter the replacement query, and another connection starts empty.
Native query lifetime across disconnects and world changes remains unverified.

Reject catalog recipes whose ingredient slots are all empty before
calling the native counter. Its admission requires a nonempty ingredient.
A placeholder must not abort the candidate loop and suppress the inventory
result. The regression ignores a placeholder, plans the following valid
recipe, and retains the untouched source inventory.

Retain an opening-scoped crafting preview independently of source stacks
and search selection. Official Bedrock 1.26.60.5/BDS 1.26.60.6 controls on
protocol2225 show unavailable Oak Planks and Stick ingredients/output
without inventory transactions or embedded/standalone stack requests.
The preview survives search edits and blank-slot focus, then clears on
reopening. A craftable control sends one accepted stack request.

Rank admitted recipes by their first ingredient-count round, preferring
the greatest minimum count and then partial ingredient coverage when no
candidate is craftable. Preserve native priority order for ties. Resolve
preview variants from source-family preferences: logs, planks, wooden
slabs and bamboo blocks in the combined player inventory contribute
prefixes before the first underscore; choose the first native string-tree
entry, or the original oak default. Concrete preview construction keeps
Block selection separate from ordinary Item construction. Original native
ranking instructions, variant callbacks and default initialization support
these rules with explicit source/constructor boundaries. Native fallback
member traversal depends on runtime Item pointers; the port retains its
connection tag-index traversal, without claiming identical fallback pixels.

Transport crafting previews separately from recipe-cell items. Version2
adds bounded crafting-slot snapshots; readers also accept version1, and
empty-preview publications retain version1 framing. A disabled primary
selection can update only this preview. It cannot authorize a placement
request or change actual inventory slots, stack IDs or the cursor. Retire
previews on opening/catalog replacement and keep them through query edits.
Boundary regressions cover owned wire snapshots, stale commands, no source
mutation/request, source-family choice and reopening.

Select ghost candidates independently of recipe unlocks. The original
1.26.60.5 candidate builder143a51800 enumerates output and station matches
through143694600/143694360, and callback143a85280 appends each match without
consulting the player unlock collection. Search exposes locked recipes.
Keep availability and request admission separate from this read-only
selection. An installed fresh Survival player with recipesUnlock=true and
no unlocked oak-planks recipes exposed the previous extra filter.

The boundary regression searches a locked recipe with an empty inventory,
publishes its ghosts, and verifies that neither a request ID nor an item
request is allocated. It also retains search/reopening and replay guards.

Keep optional crafting previews out of generation-zero publications. Pass
the exact generation captured by publish into snapshot construction so a
retiring or pending opening cannot invalidate the complete message. Skip
unrepresentable preview slots and omit a preview whose output is missing.
Retain recipe rows and search while unsupported display items remain absent.

Ignore placeholder candidates without any nonempty ingredient before
counting. Tests preserve later candidates and the no-selection result,
and cover generation-zero snapshots, unmapped preview inputs/output,
search persistence, reopening and no request allocation.

Resolve player and workbench scenes with the shared native bundle tooltip
view and transport the generic empty-description translation. Keep bundle
physical sources outside Java menu and recipe action scopes. Original
registered getter144c0aa90 dispatches through the actual player/crafting
vtable14f222710 to getter140084510 and returns true for is_container_screen.
Inherit the common classification instead of gating the tooltip subtree on
the workbench boolean. Authored-program replay tests cover both screen hosts;
input lifetime and rendered tooltip integration remain separate work.

Bind retained storage policy maps through the native descriptor matcher.
Preserve tags, name and item_tag precedence, named auxiliary suffixes and
per-definition lazy resolution even when numeric item IDs collide. Cache
bindings on the selected receiver; recipe-source locks do not control
storage filter matches. Banned descriptors override allowed descriptors.

Bedrock 1.26.60.5 protocol2225 reader141a72880 and vector converter148f31150
execute 15 map controls and one four-entry composition control. The original
default factory 141abfe00 initializes its version word to 65535. Executable
SHA256 is 988f6a61403b4381aa41e6c66d64cce441b14e561a9b10f647c2758ef3f2672a.
Name construction retains auxiliary 32767 for lazy lookup. Supplied services
capture Molang and tag construction; native compilation and the effective
post-default language context remain outside these controls.

Require an explicit supported language context for expression bindings.
Unsupported expressions make the entire policy unavailable rather than
silently removing a banned filter. The live caller has no proven context,
so it binds only name and item-tag policies. Ordinary Java bundle clicks use
those filters and nesting admission before prediction, retaining their
existing fallback when binding is unavailable. Their weight and ordering
remain separate from native physical-slot planning and request execution.

Regressions cover filter precedence, auxiliary values, tagged source stacks,
unknown expression contexts, unsupported banned expressions, retained
receiver identity and both accepted and rejected Java insertion directions.

Execute native physical-slot bundle plans through an opening-bound action
session. Resolve the root and dynamic owner from current server state,
retain the root snapshot and definition registry, and revalidate every read
container after planning. Require known insertion filters instead of using
the Java fallback for unavailable native policy. Extraction does not require
weight components that only insertion consumes. Keep this backend separate
from controller admission and Java bundle item ordinals.

Publish each Take, Place or Swap as its own predicted request in the
existing ordered transfer batch. Chain negative request IDs through changed
slots, including emptied destinations, while unchanged empty slots use zero.
Preserve preparation requests even when incoming-item admission returns
false. The session reports that native result separately from request count.

Use the physical transfer order established by original 1.26.60.5 handlers
143a28680 and 143a2c350 and the existing captured negative-ID convention.
Native wire grouping, the full gesture controller and installed routing
remain separate validation; Java boundary tests do not establish them.

Reuse request prediction and response reconciliation for accepted/rejected
batches and dynamic-owner replacement. Restore only matching predicted slots
after construction/submission failure, preserving newer server revisions
and the crafting grid of a replacement opening. Regressions execute the
actual request tracker with out-of-order acknowledgments, rejected cleanup,
empty-slot ID chaining, unavailable filters, stale contexts, failed sends,
new authoritative contents and same-object reopening.

Expose desktop default extraction separately from selected-cell extraction
through the trusted bundle session. Preserve the default helper's Take kind
when merging into an occupied cursor and return failure for a full cursor.
The request regression checks last physical selection, partial prediction,
authoritative acceptance and the following no-op without allocating a request.

Original state1 dispatcher controls distinguish desktop predicates14503b3b0/
14503b570 from the touch state45 predicates. With no retained primary source,
288 input-mode1/3, tooltip-mode0/2, phase and source cases establish activation
entry into state50 for storage insertion and state51 for default extraction.
Twenty-four original state50 controls establish selected-cursor versus clicked
source ownership and sound results, without desktop transfer flights. Admission,
source/model services and mutation results are supplied; predicate and handler
runs are separate. Full controller composition and installed routing remain
unfinished. Do not apply state45's input-mode2 restriction to state1.

Bind desktop bundle inputs to the published player/workbench opening,
generation, revision and shared monotonic command sequence. The optional
channel accepts only typed collection/index sources, primary/secondary input
and tooltip mode. Resolve roots, stacks, physical children and request IDs
from server-owned containers; client item previews cannot authorize transfers.
Retain source and nested dynamic-container snapshots, including missing owners,
and publish a fresh revision when contents or ownership change even if the
recipe presentation stays identical. Retire stale commands before mutation.

Twenty-four additional original 1.26.60.5 state1 dispatcher controls establish
that secondary input with an empty cursor enters default extraction only for
a storage target with tooltip mode2, on activation in input modes1/3. Modes0,
later phases and non-storage targets do not enter that handler. As in the
previous controls, admission and item/model services are supplied, so these
controls do not establish complete controller composition or packet grouping.

Keep request-slot ownership independent of backing-array indices: workbench
native slots32..40 occupy array entries1..9. Boundary tests cover both transfer
directions through a real workbench, rejection rollback, replay across recipe
and bundle commands, nested content/owner changes, late dynamic arrival, stale
openings and source whitelists. This transport alone does not enable add-on
bundle gestures; installed direct/proxy and sound behavior remain unverified.

Keep bundle operation outcomes through an optional native_bundle_result reply.
Older clients retain the exact v1 player-result payload. A stale or unavailable
input requests no sound; attempted insertion failure differs from silent failed
extraction. Bind sound playback to the same opening/generation/request reply.

Bedrock 1.26.60.5 executable SHA-256
988f6a61403b4381aa41e6c66d64cce441b14e561a9b10f647c2758ef3f2672a
executes state50 insertion, sound helper144fe5060 and position140ba2af0
together in24 controls. The helper dispatches the event to the actor level
at its float AABB center with data-1 and an empty selector. Protocol2225
LevelSoundEvent explicit x-enum-binary-value maps insertion446, removal447
and failed insertion533. Operation admission/mutation and final level dispatch
remain supplied boundaries; native bank selection and installed audio are separate.

Apply the same nonempty-ingredient admission to output quantity counting
as preview selection. Zero-slot and all-empty placeholders contribute no
output and cannot abort later valid candidates. A regression reproduces
the inventory count failure before this guard and retains later outputs.
